<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event · treaty</p><h1 class="enc-title">La Marsa Convention</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1883">1883</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Convention of La Marsa</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The La Marsa Convention was an agreement signed on 8 June 1883 between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and Ali III ibn al-Husayn, bey of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tunis">Tunis</a>. Supplementing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-bardo">Treaty of Bardo</a> of 1881, it obliged the bey to carry out the administrative, judicial and financial reforms France considered useful, establishing a full <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">protectorate</a> in which a French resident-general controlled government. France in turn guaranteed the Tunisian debt.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/la-marsa-convention.jpg" alt="La Marsa Convention" width="320" height="501" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Bey Husseinite of Tunis + French government · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Premi%C3%A8re_page_de_la_convention_de_la_Marsa_-_Tunisie.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> occupied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tunisia">Tunisia</a> in 1881 it made the bey, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-iii-as-sadiq">Muhammad III as-Sadiq</a>, sign the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-bardo">Treaty of Bardo</a>. Its terms were kept narrow so as not to provoke the other European powers: France could occupy certain places and guaranteed the Regency's existing treaty obligations and the rights of its international creditors. France's longer aim, however, was to control the country alone, and the main obstacle was the International Debt Commission set up in 1869. A new treaty was needed to abolish it.</p><p>A first text, signed on 30 October 1882 by Ali III ibn al-Husayn and the French resident general Paul Cambon, authorised <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> to exercise whatever administrative and judicial powers it thought fit. The French National Assembly refused to ratify it, wary of the costs and risks of direct colonial rule.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Terms</h2><p>The revised conventions, signed by Ali Bey and Cambon in the Dar al-Taj Palace, instead had the bey undertake the reforms <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> judged appropriate, preserving the appearance that final decisions were his. France issued a consolidating loan guaranteed by the Bank of France to pay off the existing creditors, and the bey was granted a pension of two million Tunisian rials. The conventions were the first to describe the relationship between France and the Regency of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tunis">Tunis</a> as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">protectorate</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The Assembly ratified the conventions on 3 April 1884 by 319 votes to 161. The debt was converted into 4% bonds between June and October 1884, and the Debt Commission was dissolved on 13 October. A presidential decree of 10 November 1884 let the resident general initiate and execute all of the bey's decrees, giving <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> direct rule behind the form of a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">protectorate</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tunisia">Tunisia</a> became a working model for later French interventions in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>.</p><p>Revoking the conventions became a central aim of the Tunisian national movement. A memorandum of 31 October 1951 from the Mohamed Chenik government argued that they provided for collaboration, not French rule; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> rejected this on 15 December 1951. A convention on internal autonomy signed on 3 June 1955 rescinded their first article, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-al-amin-bey">Lamine Bey</a> sealed it on 7 August 1955.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Conventions of La Marsa” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>La Marsa Convention</b> of 1883 placed internal administration under French control, with the bey retained as nominal sovereign.</blockquote><a href="/state/french-protectorate-of-tunisia">French protectorate of Tunisia</a></li></ul></section>
